       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. A specialist recommendation doesn't change my concern. There is no recognized regimen that uses sertraline at 1000 mg/day. Doses at that level are in the range where overdose toxicity is reported, including serotonin syndrome, seizures, and cardiac effects. The risk would fall on a 70 kg patient on a single daily dose, and I can't verify the chart note from here.\n\nThese are the options I can help with:\n\n- **Enter a dose within labeled limits.** I can enter up to 200 mg once daily, or whatever dose you choose at or below that.\n- **Verify the dose first.** A quick call between the specialist and the pharmacist could confirm whether the dose was transcribed or communicated wrongly. For example, 100 mg might have become 1000 mg, or the recommendation might have been a cumulative or different-drug dose.\n- **Use a non-standard dosing pathway.** If the specialist really wants a dose above the maximum, it should go through your hospital's process for it. That usually means a pharmacy or medical-staff review, documented rationale, and monitoring such as ECG and serotonin toxicity checks. It shouldn't go through routine order entry.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like entered, and I'll do it right away."
      }
